1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:00,800
Hi — I'm

2
00:00:00,933 --> 00:00:04,133
Divij Pawar. This video is about how much autonomy you should actually

3
00:00:04,133 --> 00:00:05,267
give an AI agent.

4
00:00:05,733 --> 00:00:08,067
Every time you give it a new permission, you're making a bet.

5
00:00:08,467 --> 00:00:11,767
Not a metaphor — an actual bet, with real stakes attached, whether

6
00:00:11,767 --> 00:00:12,967
or not you thought of it that way.

7
00:00:13,700 --> 00:00:16,267
Let it read your calendar — small bet. Let it send emails

8
00:00:16,267 --> 00:00:19,400
on your behalf — bigger bet. Let it move money, delete files,

9
00:00:19,633 --> 00:00:22,167
run code on a live system — now you're betting something you

10
00:00:22,167 --> 00:00:23,400
can't easily take back.

11
00:00:23,867 --> 00:00:25,900
This isn't a video about which agent is best.

12
00:00:26,333 --> 00:00:28,700
It's about a decision you make every time you set one up,

13
00:00:28,900 --> 00:00:30,767
usually without noticing you're making it at all.

14
00:00:31,470 --> 00:00:34,537
In engineering, there's a concept called blast radius — how much damage

15
00:00:34,537 --> 00:00:36,203
spreads outward, if something goes wrong.

16
00:00:36,803 --> 00:00:40,037
A single light bulb burning out has almost no blast radius. A

17
00:00:40,037 --> 00:00:42,837
gas leak in a sealed building has an enormous one. The mechanism

18
00:00:42,837 --> 00:00:45,737
that fails doesn't have to be dramatic, for the consequence to be.

19
00:00:46,003 --> 00:00:48,503
Every permission you give an agent sets its blast radius.

20
00:00:48,903 --> 00:00:51,503
Read-only access to your calendar — if it's wrong, you see a

21
00:00:51,503 --> 00:00:52,303
bad summary.

22
00:00:52,537 --> 00:00:55,537
Full write access to your bank account — if it's wrong, money

23
00:00:55,537 --> 00:00:56,337
moves.

24
00:00:56,203 --> 00:00:57,070
Same kind of mistake,

25
00:00:57,537 --> 00:00:58,803
wildly different consequence,

26
00:00:59,170 --> 00:01:01,037
purely because of what it was allowed to touch.

27
00:01:01,720 --> 00:01:04,320
There are three broad models for how much control you hand over,

28
00:01:04,387 --> 00:01:06,253
and real systems today sit at each point.

29
00:01:06,820 --> 00:01:09,453
Read-only. The agent can look, but it can't touch. It can tell

30
00:01:09,453 --> 00:01:12,253
you what's on your calendar, summarize your inbox, flag a conflict —

31
00:01:12,253 --> 00:01:14,653
but it cannot send, delete, or change anything itself.

32
00:01:15,253 --> 00:01:17,587
If it's wrong, the cost is your time, reading a bad answer.

33
00:01:17,920 --> 00:01:19,320
Nothing in the world actually moved.

34
00:01:19,920 --> 00:01:22,187
Approval-gated. The agent proposes an action, and stops.

35
00:01:22,553 --> 00:01:23,420
It drafts the email.

36
00:01:24,020 --> 00:01:27,287
It builds the itinerary. It writes the code change. But nothing goes

37
00:01:27,287 --> 00:01:30,453
out, nothing executes, until a person looks at it, and presses confirm.

38
00:01:30,453 --> 00:01:34,387
This is where a huge amount of real, everyday AI assistance already

39
00:01:34,387 --> 00:01:38,087
lives — drafts, suggestions, proposed changes, sitting one click away from a

40
00:01:38,087 --> 00:01:38,887
human's decision.

41
00:01:39,220 --> 00:01:42,287
Full autonomy. The agent doesn't stop and ask. It acts, and you

42
00:01:42,287 --> 00:01:43,587
find out afterward, if at all.

43
00:01:43,987 --> 00:01:45,787
An agent auto-paying recurring bills.

44
00:01:46,453 --> 00:01:47,253
An agent

45
00:01:47,287 --> 00:01:49,987
auto-deploying code straight to a live product. An agent

46
00:01:50,387 --> 00:01:53,553
auto-replying to customers, without a human reading the message first.

47
00:01:54,153 --> 00:01:56,987
This tier is the most useful, because it removes you from the

48
00:01:56,987 --> 00:02:00,553
loop entirely — and the most dangerous, for exactly the same reason.

49
00:02:01,240 --> 00:02:04,407
Here's the part that's easy to miss. Usefulness and risk don't rise

50
00:02:04,407 --> 00:02:05,573
together, at the same rate.

51
00:02:06,173 --> 00:02:09,307
Going from read-only to approval-gated buys you a lot of usefulness for

52
00:02:09,307 --> 00:02:11,873
a small amount of added risk — you get real drafts and

53
00:02:11,873 --> 00:02:14,907
real plans, and a human still catches mistakes before they matter.

54
00:02:15,407 --> 00:02:18,407
But going from approval-gated to full autonomy buys you convenience at a

55
00:02:18,407 --> 00:02:21,373
much steeper cost, because now the thing that used to catch mistakes

56
00:02:21,540 --> 00:02:23,607
— a person, looking, before anything happens

57
00:02:23,973 --> 00:02:26,873
— is gone. A financial agent that can only show you your

58
00:02:26,873 --> 00:02:30,673
spending is safe, and only modestly useful. One that drafts a payment

59
00:02:30,673 --> 00:02:33,773
for your approval is genuinely useful, and still safe, because you're the

60
00:02:33,773 --> 00:02:34,573
last check.

61
00:02:34,807 --> 00:02:37,707
One that pays automatically is the most useful of the three —

62
00:02:37,707 --> 00:02:40,140
right up until it pays the wrong amount, to the wrong account,

63
00:02:40,140 --> 00:02:42,940
and there's no draft to catch it, because there never was one.

64
00:02:43,570 --> 00:02:46,937
Delegating decisions to something else isn't a new problem. You already do

65
00:02:46,937 --> 00:02:47,737
it constantly.

66
00:02:48,103 --> 00:02:50,737
You give an employee a company card with a spending limit. You

67
00:02:50,737 --> 00:02:53,903
give a pilot's autopilot control of the plane at cruising altitude, but

68
00:02:53,903 --> 00:02:57,703
not during takeoff. You give a financial advisor power of attorney, sometimes,

69
00:02:57,703 --> 00:03:00,237
over specific accounts, within specific bounds.

70
00:03:00,637 --> 00:03:03,837
All of these are blast-radius decisions humans have been making for a

71
00:03:03,837 --> 00:03:04,637
long time.

72
00:03:05,037 --> 00:03:07,703
What's different with an AI agent is speed, and visibility.

73
00:03:08,370 --> 00:03:10,937
A human employee who's about to make a bad call usually shows

74
00:03:10,937 --> 00:03:14,203
some sign of it beforehand — hesitation, a question, a paper trail

75
00:03:14,203 --> 00:03:15,003
you could catch

76
00:03:15,470 --> 00:03:18,737
mid-process. An autonomous agent can go from decision to action in a

77
00:03:18,737 --> 00:03:21,537
fraction of a second, and the first sign anything went wrong might

78
00:03:21,537 --> 00:03:24,670
be the result itself — already irreversible — sitting in your inbox,

79
00:03:24,670 --> 00:03:25,770
or your bank statement.

80
00:03:26,450 --> 00:03:29,017
So here's where this actually leaves you. Not a rule. Not a

81
00:03:29,017 --> 00:03:32,483
checklist, this time. Just the question underneath all of it. Every agent

82
00:03:32,483 --> 00:03:35,117
you set up, every permission you grant, is you deciding how much

83
00:03:35,117 --> 00:03:37,483
rope to give something that can move faster than you can watch.

84
00:03:38,050 --> 00:03:40,617
Read-only gives it almost no rope, and almost no reach.

85
00:03:41,017 --> 00:03:43,383
Full autonomy gives it all the rope, and all the reach that

86
00:03:43,383 --> 00:03:46,950
comes with it. Most of what actually matters happens in between —

87
00:03:46,983 --> 00:03:50,083
in how carefully you choose the approval gates, and in being honest

88
00:03:50,083 --> 00:03:53,283
with yourself about what you'd lose, if the rope ran out at

89
00:03:53,283 --> 00:03:54,550
the worst possible moment.

90
00:03:55,250 --> 00:03:58,017
So — read-only gives it almost no rope, and almost no reach.

91
00:03:58,517 --> 00:04:01,383
Approval-gated keeps a person as the last check, and buys you most

92
00:04:01,383 --> 00:04:03,617
of the usefulness for very little of the added risk.

93
00:04:04,117 --> 00:04:07,150
Full autonomy removes you from the loop entirely, which is exactly what

94
00:04:07,150 --> 00:04:09,517
makes it the most useful and the most dangerous, at the same

95
00:04:09,517 --> 00:04:10,317
time.

96
00:04:10,417 --> 00:04:12,483
Usefulness climbs steadily across all three.

97
00:04:12,850 --> 00:04:15,450
Risk stays flat, and then bends hard at the last step.

98
00:04:16,017 --> 00:04:18,183
That's not a question with one correct answer.

99
00:04:18,717 --> 00:04:22,483
It's a question worth actually asking, every single time, before you hand

100
00:04:22,483 --> 00:04:23,417
something the keys.

101
00:04:24,160 --> 00:04:27,293
Your turn. Here's the prompt: "Map the blast radius of every permission

102
00:04:27,293 --> 00:04:28,693
I've granted an AI agent."

103
00:04:29,460 --> 00:04:31,927
Run it against the tools already wired into your accounts — your

104
00:04:31,927 --> 00:04:33,993
mail, your calendar, your repositories, your cards.

105
00:04:34,393 --> 00:04:37,360
Then check the answer three ways. Does every permission land clearly in

106
00:04:37,393 --> 00:04:40,327
read, approve, or act? Does it name what specifically moves in the

107
00:04:40,327 --> 00:04:41,927
world if that permission fires wrong?

108
00:04:42,327 --> 00:04:44,760
And does it say what you would lose, concretely, on the riskiest

109
00:04:44,760 --> 00:04:45,560
one?

110
00:04:45,493 --> 00:04:48,327
If what comes back is only a list of features, ask again.

111
00:04:48,827 --> 00:04:51,160
You want the blast radius, not the feature set.

112
00:04:51,870 --> 00:04:52,670
Signing off — Divij Pawar.
